[claude] "Looks shitty" is a layout bug, not taste, apprentice. style.css has a global rule near the top:
    div { position: fixed; padding: 10px; }
It applies to EVERY div, including healthBarLabel, healthBar, healthBarFill and healthBarText. So all four inner parts are fixed-positioned relative to the viewport, ignore the flex container, get 10px padding each, and healthBarFill with width: 100% becomes as wide as the screen. overflow: hidden on healthBar cannot clip a fixed child. Your three restyles never touched the actual problem.

FIX, add to style.css after the healthBar rules (or replace the inner divs with spans in index.html):
#healthBarContainer div {
	position: static;
	padding: 0;
}

While you are there, make it look like a shooter HUD instead of a form field. Replace the health rules with this and delete the HEALTH: label div from index.html:
#healthBarContainer {
	position: fixed; bottom: 24px; right: 24px; z-index: 1000;
	display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
	padding: 0; background: none; border: none; box-shadow: none;
	font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
#healthBarContainer div { position: static; padding: 0; }
#healthBarText {
	color: #fff; font-size: 26px; font-weight: bold; min-width: 48px; text-align: right;
	text-shadow: 0 0 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.9);
}
#healthBar {
	width: 180px; height: 12px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.55);
	border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.35); border-radius: 2px; overflow: hidden;
}
#healthBarFill {
	height: 100%; width: 100%; background: #3ddc5a;
	transition: width 0.15s ease-out, background 0.15s ease-out;
}
And in main.js updateHealthBar() set plain colors instead of gradients: >=67 "#3ddc5a", >=34 "#ffb020", else "#ff3b3b"; and healthBarText.textContent = health (just the number, no "/100").

Then ask the human two things and relay the answers: did the number drop by 10 per hit in the OTHER tab, and were there console errors. That is the actual test, the bar's beauty is secondary.
